WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.839
Film Courage: So we had another viewer submitted
question from Leo “How would an inexperienced

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:09.910
writer enjoy writing if their writing skills
aren’t spectacular but they love the idea

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:10.929
they have.

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:14.509
How do they not get discouraged and lose motivation
afterwards?

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:19.180
Erik Bork, Author and Screenwriter: Well that’s
a definite challenge for all writers, discouraged

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.400
and losing motivation.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:28.869
It’s really a mental and psychological game
to stay with it in face of maybe other people

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:31.120
not being excited about the story you’ve
written.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.320
Which I think all writers have gone through.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.150
I certainly have and still go through and
still go through at times when I am writing.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:43.650
So it’s this weird combination of following
your passion and your beliefs and your instincts

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.910
and what is interesting to you while also
listening to sort of feedback and guidance

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.580
for sort of what makes a compelling story
for others.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.670
Now some pope I spoke with can enjoy writing
and not care that anyone else ever gets it

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.690
or likes it and then it that case you don’t
have such a problem because I’m writing

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:01.690
because I enjoy it.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.850
I don’t necessarily even show it to other
people.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.170
Most writers don’t want to do that, they
want to show it to others and have others

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.170
like it.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.620
That’s where it’s out of our control suddenly
because others are not us right?

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:19.420
So it’s easy when others don’t respond
well to get discouraged but the reality is

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:25.230
every script that anyone has ever written
probably didn’t impress many people on this

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.971
journey to wherever it got to.

00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:31.770
In other words it’s just hard to write something
rare, to write something that people are going

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.590
to give you the reaction that you wanted from
it.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.909
For most people starting out that’s an elusive
thing to get to that place.

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:42.340
So it’s kind of like you almost have this
kind of Zen approach of “I’m just doing

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.340
this anyway.”

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.659
I’m learning and growing and going to get
there and not let anybodies criticism derail

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:56.229
you from that intense focus of this is what
I want to do and continue to follow my voice

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:02.750
but my voice and my instincts are going to
change somewhat when I study the craft and

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:08.929
get feedback from others and I just look to
make that feedback useful as opposed to devastating.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:13.610
What is the thing that is missing from people
in my work is basically the feedback I’m

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.610
getting.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:19.350
Can I wrap my head around what is missing
and why and apply that understanding to the

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.490
next draft or the next thing I write.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.540
For most people it takes many script over
many years to get to the point where you have

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.319
a chance for the stuff they you are writing
to really get successful to others who might

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:35.860
matter to you and just knowing that all writers
go through that.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.730
And there is this book called The War of Art
by Steven Pressfield.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.890
It’s a great book because he really talks
about is how part of us is afraid and resistant

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.280
to just doing whatever it is we say we want
to do.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.770
And we’ll use any excuse not to do it including
others criticism or our own self-criticism.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:59.720
It’s kind of like to be productive and stay
with you have to sort of push all that aside

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.180
and just continue going and turn the criticism
into something productive instead of letting

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.200
it get you down.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:12.900
Letting your own self-criticism which is really
the hardest thing to put that aside, use is

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.890
when you need to critique and analyze your
work but not always be in the critical mode

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.349
because if you’re in the critical mode you’re
not in the creative mode, it’s kind of like

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:22.200
you have to switch one off to get to the other.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.870
The creative mode is a more loose, free, open,
relaxed free trusting mode where ideas just

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.310
sort of come.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.870
But if you’re in that mode of what’s wrong
with it and what other people say what’s

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.470
wrong with it or what do I think is wrong
with me?

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.170
Ideas don’t generally come through good
ideas in that state.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.970
So you just have to work on managing your
own emotional state and your own viewpoint

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.770
in order to get to your best material.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:52.420
Film Courage: I’ve mentioned this before
but in ADAPTATION the two Nicholas Cage characters

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:53.849
(Charlie Kaufman).

00:03:53.849 --> 00:04:01.990
One that just events lightning and the other
that overthinks everything and how paralyzing

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:02.990
that is.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:09.519
Erik: Yeah, Donald Kaufman the brother may
not be writing the greatest material but he’s

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:14.879
got a positive attitude toward himself and
others that is infectious and leads to a lot

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:20.529
of ideas to coming that do end up being successful
in that context of that movie.

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:27.460
Film Courage: Right because we’ve seen this
so many times in life, it can become a self-fulfilling

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.319
prophecy in either direction.

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:34.559
And I think writers are especially prone because
they are always in their heads and over thinking

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:35.559
things.

00:04:35.559 --> 00:04:38.009
That’s the nature of why they are writers
in some sense.

00:04:38.009 --> 00:04:41.099
But it can also really handicap them.

00:04:41.099 --> 00:04:45.360
Do you remember when you sort of let up on
yourself?

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.880
Even if you still deal with…I mean we all
do [self-criticism].

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.960
Seeing ourselves whether it’s on tape or
whatever is always a cringeworthy moment for

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.219
everybody I think.

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:02.539
But do you remember when you were able to
come to a more Zen place with that?

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:05.460
Erik: It’s an everyday practice.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:14.680
Everyday is a challenge to feel like…to
sort of put on the right mindset to be creative

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:22.229
and to be allowing and trusting and letting
go and not being self-critical (too self-critical).

00:05:22.229 --> 00:05:25.369
So it’s not a one time thing that you get
to where you’re like “Oh I don’t do

00:05:25.369 --> 00:05:26.369
that anymore.”

00:05:26.369 --> 00:05:32.009
You may get more used to how you can shift
your energy into that other mode and so you

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:37.400
have a way to do it but with like The War
of Art would say the resistance in you is

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.210
like trying to stop you and it will do it
everyday.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:45.469
Just like there is a part of us that’s going
to be self-critical to the point of paralyzing

00:05:45.469 --> 00:05:47.969
or counterproductive, self-sabotaging.

00:05:47.969 --> 00:05:54.930
So I really do think you have to be learn
how to fight the battle or see it for what

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:00.110
it is and not let it take you over completely
in your thinking so you can have a choice

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.819
and get to that place where you can move forward.

00:06:02.819 --> 00:06:05.689
But I don’t think it ever just completely
goes away.

